<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yaroslav II of Vladimir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1191">1191</a>–<a href="/years/1246">1246</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Yaroslav Vsevolodovich · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yaroslav II of Vladimir, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vsevolod-the-big-nest">Vsevolod the Big Nest</a>, held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereslavl-zalessky">Pereslavl-Zalessky</a> from 1212 and was grand prince of Vladimir from 1238 to 1246, after the Mongol invasion. He was the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a>, born at Pereslavl. He died in 1246 after a journey to the Mongol court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a>, after which his sons received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanages</a> including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kostroma">Kostroma</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yaroslav-ii-of-vladimir.jpg" alt="Yaroslav II of Vladimir" width="320" height="446" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yaroslav_II_Vsevolodovich_grand_duke_of_Vladimir.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yaroslav Vsevolodovich, baptised Theodore, was born on 8 February 1191, the fourth son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vsevolod-the-big-nest">Vsevolod the Big Nest</a> and Maria Shvarnovna. In 1200 his father sent him to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereiaslav">Pereiaslav</a> near the Kypchak steppes. Six years later the boyars of Halych summoned him to their city, but he could not claim the throne. He was then sent to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a>, and the stubborn opposition of its inhabitants led to the city being burnt. In 1209 Vsevolod sent him against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mstislav-mstislavich">Mstislav the Bold</a> in Novgorod, and after several battles the two made peace and Yaroslav married Mstislav's daughter.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On his deathbed Vsevolod left him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereslavl-zalessky">Pereslavl-Zalessky</a>. In the quarrel between his elder brothers Konstantin and Yuri he supported Yuri. In 1215 he accepted an offer from the Novgorodians to be their prince, but he captured Torzhok and blocked its grain from Novgorod. He was defeated by his father-in-law on the Lipitsa River, and a helmet he lost there was found by archaeologists in 1808. He returned to Novgorod in 1234, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> some years later, and in 1236 moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> on the advice of Daniel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, leaving his son Alexander as his representative in the north.</p><p>In 1238, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> first invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> and Yuri was killed in battle, Yaroslav left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> for Vladimir, where he was crowned grand prince. He tried to restore the cities of Vladimir-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> after the ravages of the Mongol invasion. In 1243 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a> summoned him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a>, and after a long conference he returned with honours. Two years later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guyuk-khan">Güyük Khan</a> summoned him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to Giovanni da Pian del Carpine, Yaroslav ate at the tent of Töregene Khatun, fell ill at once, and died within a week, on 30 September 1246. Carpine reports that everyone believed Töregene had poisoned him in order to conquer his lands, though later historians have disputed the accusation. By his third wife, Fedosia Igorevna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a>, whom he married in 1218, he had at least twelve children, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrei-ii-of-vladimir">Andrei II of Vladimir</a>, Mikhail Khorobrit, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-of-tver">Yaroslav of Tver</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-of-kostroma">Vasily of Kostroma</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yaroslav II of Vladimir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The invasion of 1237–1238 then wrecked its towns and armies and ended its independence: from 1243, when <b>Yaroslav II</b> accepted the patent of Khan Batu, the grand prince of Vladimir was a vassal of the Golden Horde.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vladimir-suzdal">Vladimir-Suzdal · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1227 Prince <b>Yaroslav Vsevolodovich</b> of Novgorod had many Karelians baptised into Orthodox Christianity, and Korela became their main fortified centre.</blockquote><a href="/state/karelians">Karelians</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1246 it became the appanage of Konstantin, son of Grand Prince <b>Yaroslav Vsevolodovich</b>, and his descendants held it for over a century.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-galich-mersky">Principality of Galich-Mersky</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1246, after the death of <b>Yaroslav Vsevolodovich</b>, his youngest son Vasily received Kostroma as his own appanage.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-kostroma">Principality of Kostroma</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Yaroslav Vsevolodovich</b> held it from 1212, and his son Alexander Nevsky was born there around 1220.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-pereslavl-zalessky">Principality of Pereslavl-Zalessky</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereslavl-zalessky">Pereslavl-Zalessky</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.74,38.86&amp;year=1226" aria-label="Pereslavl-Zalessky on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5389 237 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Yaroslav II of Vladimir"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5839 507h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5839 507h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56.74,38.86&amp;year=1226">What was here in 1226? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
